      <h1 class="ed-title">How Long Should Seniors Walk on a Treadmill?</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        The target for most adults over 65 is about 30 minutes of moderate walking on five days a week, adding up to 150 minutes. But almost nobody should start there. Begin with 5 to 10 minutes and add a few minutes a week.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 19, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS<br />
          <span class="ed-method">Based on published exercise guidance. General information, not medical advice.</span>
        </div>
        <a href="#short-answer" class="btn-ghost">The Short Answer</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        Nothing on this page is a paid placement, and it carries no affiliate links. <a href="/editorial-standards/">How we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          "How long should a 70-year-old walk on a treadmill?" has a clear answer at the end point
          and a much more personal one at the start. Public health guidance gives older adults a
          weekly target, and it is the same target younger adults get. What changes with age is
          how carefully you get there, what else belongs in the week alongside walking, and how
          to tell a normal tired feeling from a reason to stop. This page covers all of that,
          specifically for older walkers.
        </p>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="On this page">
          <h2>On this page</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#guidelines">What the CDC and WHO Recommend for Older Adults</a></li>
            <li><a href="#by-starting-point">How Long to Walk, by Where You Are Starting</a></li>
            <li><a href="#build-up">An Eight-Week Build-Up From 5 to 30 Minutes</a></li>
            <li><a href="#splitting">Splitting Walks Into Shorter Sessions</a></li>
            <li><a href="#how-often">How Often to Walk, and When to Rest</a></li>
            <li><a href="#steps">How Many Steps Should a Senior Aim For?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#strength-balance">Why Walking Time Is Not the Whole Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#signs-to-stop">Signs to Slow Down or Stop</a></li>
            <li><a href="#too-long">Can a Senior Walk Too Long?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#verdict">The Verdict</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Most seniors, including people in their seventies, should build up to about 150
            minutes of moderate-intensity walking a week — for example, 30 minutes on five days —
            starting with sessions of just 5 to 10 minutes and adding a few minutes each
            week.</strong> "Moderate" means you are breathing more quickly but can still talk in
            full sentences. Split the time into shorter walks if that is easier; it all counts.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>New to exercise or returning after illness:</strong> 5 to 10 minutes, once
            or twice a day, on most days. Build from there.</li>
            <li><strong>Moderately active already:</strong> 15 to 20 minutes per session on five
            days a week, building toward 30.</li>
            <li><strong>The weekly target:</strong> 150 minutes of moderate walking, or more if you
            enjoy it and feel well.</li>
            <li><strong>Alongside the walking:</strong> muscle-strengthening on at least two days a
            week and some balance practice, which the treadmill does not provide on its own.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you have a heart condition, lung disease, diabetes, a balance problem, a recent
            fall or joint surgery, or if you have been inactive for a long time, talk to your
            doctor before starting, and ask what pace and duration suit you. For the general
            version of this question, which covers weight loss and longer walks for all ages, see <a href="/how-long-should-i-walk-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long to walk on a treadmill</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="guidelines" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What the CDC and WHO Recommend for Older Adults</h2>
<p>
            Both the US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention and the World Health
            Organization publish physical activity guidance for adults aged 65 and over. They are
            closely aligned, and both have three parts, not one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Aerobic activity.</strong> The CDC recommends that adults 65 and older get at
            least 150 minutes a week of moderate-intensity activity, such as brisk walking, or 75
            minutes of vigorous activity, or an equivalent mix. The WHO's 2020 guidelines give the
            same aerobic range as for younger adults: 150 to 300 minutes of moderate activity a
            week, or 75 to 150 minutes of vigorous activity, with additional benefits from doing
            more.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Muscle strengthening.</strong> The CDC recommends activities that strengthen
            muscles on at least two days a week, working all the major muscle groups — legs, hips,
            back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Balance.</strong> The CDC also recommends activities that improve balance,
            giving examples such as walking heel to toe and standing up from a seated position.
            The WHO is more specific: it recommends that older adults do varied, multicomponent
            physical activity that emphasises functional balance and strength training at
            moderate or greater intensity, on three or more days a week, to improve function and
            prevent falls.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The line that matters most for beginners.</strong> Both organisations say that
            older adults who cannot meet these targets because of health conditions should be as
            physically active as their abilities and conditions allow. In other words, the 150
            minutes is a destination, not an entry requirement. Ten minutes a day is far better
            than nothing, and moving from inactive to a little active is where much of the
            benefit lies.
          </p>
          <p>
            For treadmill walking, that translates into a simple picture: around 30 minutes of
            moderate walking on five days covers the aerobic part; two short strength sessions and
            some balance practice on other days, or after a walk, cover the rest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-starting-point" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How Long to Walk, by Where You Are Starting</h2>
<p>
            The right session length today depends far more on your current activity level than
            on your age. A 78-year-old who has walked a dog every day for ten years and a
            66-year-old who has been mostly sitting since a hospital stay need very different
            starting points.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[560px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Starting point</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">First sessions</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">How often</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Aim for after 8 to 12 weeks</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Largely inactive, recovering from illness, or unsteady</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 minutes at an easy pace</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Once or twice a day, most days</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 to 15 minutes, two or three times a day, or a single 20 to 30 minute walk</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Some regular activity (gardening, short walks)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 minutes, the middle part at a moderate pace</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4 to 5 days a week</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">30 minutes on five days</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Already walking 20+ minutes most days</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15 to 20 minutes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 days a week</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">30 to 45 minutes on five days, with some gentle incline</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            These are our suggestions, built from the guidelines above and the usual advice to
            increase exercise gradually; they are not a medical prescription. Your doctor or
            physical therapist may advise something different, particularly after surgery or a
            cardiac event, and their advice takes priority.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Whatever the starting point, the first few sessions are partly about the
            machine.</strong> Even experienced walkers need a few sessions to get used to a moving
            belt. Spend them at a slow speed — 1 to 1.5 mph — before you worry about duration or
            effort. Our guide to <a href="/treadmill-speed-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill speed for seniors</a> explains how to find your working pace once the belt feels familiar, with typical
            walking speeds by age.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="build-up" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>An Eight-Week Build-Up From 5 to 30 Minutes</h2>
<p>
            This plan suits someone starting from little or no regular exercise. Each session
            includes a <a href="/treadmill-warm-up-and-cool-down/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>warm-up and cool-down</a> of two to three minutes at an easy pace; the minutes
            listed are the total time on the belt. "Moderate" means breathing faster but able to
            talk in sentences.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Week 1:</strong> 5 to 10 minutes at an easy pace, on four or five days. No
            moderate effort yet. Practise getting on, changing speed and getting off.</li>
            <li><strong>Week 2:</strong> 10 minutes, with the middle 4 to 5 minutes at a moderate
            pace, five days.</li>
            <li><strong>Week 3:</strong> 12 to 15 minutes, with 8 to 10 minutes moderate, five
            days.</li>
            <li><strong>Week 4:</strong> 15 minutes, with about 10 minutes moderate. Hold here if
            weeks 2 and 3 felt like hard work. A repeated week is not a setback.</li>
            <li><strong>Week 5:</strong> 18 to 20 minutes, 13 to 15 moderate.</li>
            <li><strong>Week 6:</strong> 20 to 22 minutes, about 16 moderate.</li>
            <li><strong>Week 7:</strong> 25 minutes, about 20 moderate.</li>
            <li><strong>Week 8:</strong> 30 minutes, about 24 moderate, on five days. That is close
            to the 150-minute weekly target once warm-ups are included.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>Three rules keep this safe.</strong> First, increase either time or effort in
            a given week, not both. Second, if a week leaves you sore for more than a day or two,
            or more tired than usual the next morning, repeat it rather than moving on. Third, add
            incline only once you can walk 20 minutes comfortably, and then only 1% at a time.
          </p>
          <p>
            Many people take ten or twelve weeks rather than eight, and that is entirely
            reasonable. The guideline target is the same whether you reach it in two months or
            four, and the people who get there slowly tend to still be walking a year later. For
            structured sessions to use once you have built the base, see our <a href="/treadmill-workout-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill workouts for seniors</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="splitting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Splitting Walks Into Shorter Sessions</h2>
<p>
            You do not have to walk 30 minutes in one go. Earlier editions of US guidance said
            activity had to come in bouts of at least 10 minutes to count; the current US
            Physical Activity Guidelines for Americans, revised in 2018, dropped that requirement
            and say that any amount of moderate-to-vigorous activity counts toward the weekly
            total.
          </p>
          <p>
            For older walkers, splitting the day has practical advantages:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Less fatigue per session.</strong> Three 10-minute walks are easier on
            the legs, the feet and the back than one 30-minute walk, and fatigue is when stumbles
            become more likely.</li>
            <li><strong>Easier on arthritic joints.</strong> Many people with knee or hip
            osteoarthritis find that pain builds with continuous walking but settles with short
            breaks. Our guide to <a href="/treadmill-walking-with-arthritis/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill walking with arthritis</a> covers this in more detail.</li>
            <li><strong>It breaks up sitting.</strong> A short walk after each meal interrupts long
            stretches in a chair, which has benefits of its own, and some research suggests <a href="/treadmill-walking-for-diabetes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>walking after meals</a> can help blunt rises in blood sugar.</li>
            <li><strong>It fits around energy levels.</strong> Many older adults feel best at a
            particular time of day. Walking then, in shorter pieces, beats forcing a long session
            at a bad time.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The one thing to keep in each short walk is a proper warm-up and cool-down. A
            10-minute walk might be two minutes easy, six minutes moderate and two minutes easy.
            That is still worth doing, and still counts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-often" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How Often to Walk, and When to Rest</h2>
<p>
            The CDC's 150-minute target is easiest to reach by walking on five days a week, but it
            can be spread however suits you. A few points help when planning the week.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walking is low-impact enough to do on most days.</strong> Moderate walking
            places relatively modest loads on the body, and many older adults walk daily without
            trouble. Spreading the minutes across the week is generally better than cramming
            them into two long sessions, partly because the benefits of activity on things like
            blood sugar and blood pressure are partly short-lived, and partly because shorter
            sessions are easier to recover from.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Build in at least one or two easier days.</strong> These do not have to be
            days off. An easy 10-minute stroll at your warm-up speed, or a day given over to
            strength and balance work instead, lets tired muscles and joints recover while keeping
            the habit going.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Take a full rest day when your body asks for one.</strong> Signs you need one
            include muscle soreness that has not eased after a day, aching joints the morning
            after a walk, poor sleep, or a general heaviness that makes a familiar session feel
            much harder than usual. Older muscles and tendons tend to take a little longer to
            recover than younger ones.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>After a break, step back.</strong> If illness, travel or bad weather keeps you
            off the treadmill for a week or more, restart at about half your previous session
            length and build back up over a week or two. Trying to pick up exactly where you left
            off is a common route to soreness and discouragement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="steps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How Many Steps Should a Senior Aim For?</h2>
<p>
            Many older adults track steps rather than minutes, and the research here is
            encouraging, because it suggests the well-known 10,000-step goal is more than most
            older people need for the longevity benefits studied so far.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The largest pooled analysis</strong> is a 2022 meta-analysis in <em>The Lancet
            Public Health</em> led by Amanda Paluch, which combined 15 cohort studies covering
            about 47,500 adults. Among adults aged 60 and over, the risk of death during
            follow-up fell as daily steps rose, levelling off at roughly 6,000 to 8,000 steps a
            day. For adults under 60 the levelling-off point was higher, at around 8,000 to
            10,000.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A study of older women</strong> published in <em>JAMA Internal Medicine</em> in 2019, led by I-Min Lee and drawing on about 16,700 women with an average age of 72,
            found that as few as about 4,400 steps a day was associated with lower mortality than
            about 2,700 steps a day, with the benefit continuing to rise until about 7,500 steps
            a day, after which it levelled off.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>How to use those figures honestly.</strong> Both are observational studies:
            they show that people who took more steps tended to live longer, not that adding steps
            will produce a particular result for you. Healthier people naturally walk more, and
            the researchers adjusted for this as well as they could, but cannot remove it
            entirely. Even so, the direction is consistent across studies: more daily movement is
            better, the biggest gains come at the low end, and there is no evidence an older adult
            must reach 10,000 steps.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Translating that to the treadmill.</strong> Step count per minute depends on
            your height and speed, but at a moderate pace many older adults take somewhere around
            90 to 110 steps a minute. A 30-minute treadmill walk therefore adds very roughly 3,000
            steps, which, on top of ordinary daily movement, takes many people into the 6,000 to
            8,000 range. Our explainer on <a href="/how-many-steps-in-a-mile-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how many steps are in a mile</a> covers stride length and cadence, and whether a wrist tracker counts treadmill steps
            accurately when you are <a href="/should-you-hold-the-handrails-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>holding the rails</a> (often it does not).
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="strength-balance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Why Walking Time Is Not the Whole Answer</h2>
<p>
            It is easy to read "150 minutes" and stop there, but for older adults the strength
            and balance parts of the guidelines are just as important, and treadmill walking does
            little for either.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Strength.</strong> Muscle mass and strength decline with age, and leg strength
            is closely tied to the ability to climb stairs, rise from a chair and recover from a
            trip. Walking keeps the legs working but does not load them heavily enough to build
            much strength. Two short sessions a week — sit-to-stands from a chair, wall push-ups,
            step-ups on a low stair, light dumbbells or <a href="/best-resistance-bands/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>resistance bands</a> — cover the CDC's
            recommendation. A physical therapist can set exercises appropriate to your joints.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Balance.</strong> Falls are a leading cause of injury in older adults, and
            balance improves with practice. Simple exercises such as standing on one leg near a
            counter, heel-to-toe walking along a hallway, and slow sideways steps are the kind of
            thing the CDC has in mind. Tai chi has good evidence behind it for fall prevention.
            Do these off the treadmill, on stable ground, with something sturdy to hold.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>How it fits together.</strong> A realistic week for a 70-year-old might be 25
            to 30 minutes of treadmill walking on five days, with ten minutes of strength
            exercises after two of the walks and a few minutes of balance practice most days. That
            covers all three parts of the guidance in well under an hour a day. Our page on <a href="/is-a-treadmill-good-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether a treadmill is good for seniors</a> looks at where a treadmill fits among other options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="signs-to-stop" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Signs to Slow Down or Stop</h2>
<p>
            Knowing when to cut a session short matters more than hitting a duration. Some
            sensations are normal parts of exercise; some are warnings.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Normal, and no reason to stop:</strong> breathing faster, feeling warm and
            sweating a little, legs feeling tired toward the end of a session, mild muscle
            soreness a day later that eases within a day or two.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Slow down or end the session early</strong> if you cannot talk in sentences,
            if your steps become uneven or you start to scuff or catch a toe, if you find yourself
            gripping the handrails to keep up, or if a joint begins to hurt sharply rather than
            ache. Fatigue that changes the way you walk is the point at which falls become more
            likely, so stop before you reach it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Stop and get medical help</strong> for chest pain, pressure or tightness;
            discomfort spreading to the arm, neck, jaw or back; breathlessness far beyond what the
            effort should cause; a racing, pounding or irregular heartbeat; dizziness,
            lightheadedness or fainting; or sudden weakness, numbness, confusion or trouble
            speaking. Call 911 if symptoms are severe or do not settle quickly with rest. Do not
            resume exercising until you have spoken to a doctor.
          </p>
          <p>
            To stop safely, slow the belt gradually unless you feel faint, in which case press stop
            or pull the safety key, keep hold of the rails and sit down as soon as you can. Our <a href="/treadmill-safety-tips/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety tips</a> cover the set-up details — safety key, clearance behind the deck, footwear — that make
            an unplanned stop much less risky.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="too-long" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Can a Senior Walk Too Long?</h2>
<p>
            Yes, though it is rarer than walking too little. The WHO notes additional benefits
            from going beyond 300 minutes of moderate activity a week, and many fit older adults
            walk an hour a day without trouble. The problems arise when volume rises faster than
            the body adapts.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Overuse aches.</strong> Shin pain, heel pain (often plantar fasciitis),
            and aching knees or hips after long sessions are usually signs of doing too much too
            soon. Cut back by a third for a week and build again more slowly.</li>
            <li><strong>Fatigue and footing.</strong> Long sessions at a steady speed can lull you
            into a shuffle as tiredness sets in. If your stride shortens noticeably in the last
            ten minutes, end sessions earlier or split them.</li>
            <li><strong>Heat and fluids.</strong> There is no breeze on a treadmill. Older adults
            can be less aware of thirst, so keep water within reach and a fan running for walks
            over 20 minutes.</li>
            <li><strong>Crowding out strength work.</strong> If walking takes all the time you have
            set aside for exercise, the strength and balance sessions tend to disappear. An extra
            15 minutes on the belt is worth less than 15 minutes of leg strength exercises for
            most older adults who are already walking 150 minutes a week.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you want to walk longer than about 45 minutes in one go, make sure your treadmill
            is up to it: a smooth belt at low speeds, a deck long enough for a relaxed stride and
            sturdy handrails. Our roundup of the <a href="/best-treadmills-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for seniors</a> compares machines on those points.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Verdict</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Build toward about 30 minutes of moderate treadmill walking on five days a week,
            totalling around 150 minutes, starting with 5 to 10 minutes and adding a few minutes a
            week.</strong> That is the aerobic target the CDC and WHO set for adults over 65, and it
            applies to a 70-year-old just as it does to a 50-year-old. Split the time into shorter
            walks whenever that is easier; every minute counts.
          </p>
          <p>
            Pair the walking with strength exercises on at least two days and some balance
            practice, take easier days when your body asks for them, and stop for any chest pain,
            dizziness or unusual breathlessness. If you have a heart, lung, balance or joint
            condition, or are starting after a long inactive spell, agree a plan with your doctor
            first.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>4 Mistakes Seniors Make With Treadmill Walking Time</h2>
          <p>Most of these come from treating the weekly target as a starting point rather than a destination.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Starting with 30 minutes on day one</h3>
              <p>Jumping straight to the guideline session length is the commonest way to end up with sore shins, aching knees or a fall caused by tired legs. Start with 5 to 10 minutes, add a few minutes a week, and repeat any week that leaves you sore for more than a day or two.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Counting a slow stroll as moderate activity</h3>
              <p>The 150-minute target refers to moderate effort, where breathing is faster but you can still talk. A gentle stroll is still worth doing, but it does not do the same job. Use the talk test to check your pace, and add a gentle incline rather than speed if you need more effort.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Skipping strength and balance work</h3>
              <p>Treadmill walking covers the aerobic guideline but does little for leg strength or balance, which both the CDC and WHO recommend for older adults. Two short strength sessions a week and a few minutes of balance practice on stable ground matter as much as extra walking minutes.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Pushing through warning signs</h3>
              <p>Finishing a planned 30 minutes is never worth walking through chest discomfort, dizziness, an irregular heartbeat or a toe that keeps catching. Slow down when your stride changes, stop for any warning symptom, and speak to a doctor before resuming.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The takeaway</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Build toward about 30 minutes of moderate walking on five days a
            week, starting with 5 to 10 minutes.</strong> That meets the 150-minute weekly target the
            CDC and WHO set for adults over 65, and shorter walks spread through the day count just
            as well.
          </p>
          <p>
            Add strength exercises twice a week and some balance practice, take easier days when you
            need them, and stop for any warning signs. Find your working pace with our guide to <a href="/treadmill-speed-for-seniors/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmill speed for seniors</a>,
            and check with your doctor first if you have a heart, balance or joint condition.
          </p>
        </div>

