        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>How long should a 70 year old walk on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Build toward about 30 minutes of moderate walking on five days a week, which meets the CDC's 150-minute weekly target for adults 65 and over. Start with 5 to 10 minutes if you are new to exercise and add a few minutes a week. Splitting the time into two or three shorter walks is fine.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How long should seniors walk on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The goal for most older adults is 150 minutes a week of moderate-intensity walking, such as 30 minutes on five days. Beginners should start with 5 to 10 minutes per session and build up over two to three months. The CDC and WHO also recommend strength exercises on at least two days a week and balance practice.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is 20 minutes on a treadmill enough for a senior?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Twenty minutes of moderate walking a day is a solid amount and, done daily, gives 140 minutes a week — close to the 150-minute guideline. If 20 minutes is your current limit, it is well worth doing; both the CDC and WHO say older adults should be as active as their abilities allow.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How many steps a day should a 70 year old take?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Research suggests fewer than the familiar 10,000. A 2022 Lancet Public Health meta-analysis found mortality risk in adults 60 and over levelled off at roughly 6,000 to 8,000 steps a day, and a 2019 study of women averaging 72 found benefits starting at about 4,400 steps and levelling off around 7,500. These studies show associations, not guarantees.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How often should seniors walk on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Five days a week is the simplest way to reach 150 minutes, and many older adults walk daily. Include one or two easier days, or use them for strength and balance work, and take a full rest day if muscles or joints are still sore from the previous session.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can seniors split treadmill walking into shorter sessions?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. The 2018 Physical Activity Guidelines for Americans removed the old rule that activity had to come in 10-minute bouts, so any moderate walking counts toward the weekly total. Three 10-minute walks are often easier on the joints and balance than one 30-minute walk.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

